    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2). Here's what I changed to implement soft-delete with low structural impact:\n\n**Model / persistence**\n- `model/Owner.java` \u2014 added a `deleted` field (`Boolean`, defaults to `FALSE`) with a getter/setter, as a small cohesive unit alongside the other columns.\n- `db/hsqldb/schema.sql` \u2014 added a `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE` column (the test profile is `hsqldb` with `ddl-auto=none`; the `h2` profile uses `ddl-auto=update` so it self-syncs, and `postgres`/`mysql` schemas are already stale/unmaintained).\n- `db/hsqldb/data.sql` \u2014 appended `, FALSE` to the 10 positional owner seed inserts so their column count matches.\n\n**API contract**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` response schema. MapStruct auto-maps it in both directions; `OwnerFields` (create input) is untouched, so a newly created owner keeps the model default `false`.\n\n**Behavior**\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1.deleteOwner` \u2014 instead of `deleteOwner(owner)`, it now sets `deleted=true` and `saveOwner(owner)`, retaining the row and returning `204`. GET still finds it (no filtering there) and reports `deleted: true`.\n- `OwnerUniqueIdentityAdvice` and `OwnerHouseholdDuplicateAdvice` \u2014 each got a one-line `.filter(o -> !Boolean.TRUE.equals(o.getDeleted()))` in their existing stream, so the duplicate/identity checks skip soft-deleted owners.\n\nEach touched function stays simple and I avoided editing the large `ClinicServiceImpl` (its soft-delete lever was the controller instead, whose surrounding complexity is far smaller).",
